**Read:** [Cleanroom programming and energy — Tschudi, Sartor and Xu](../sources/Cleanroom-Programming-and-Energy-LBNL.lingua#r_36R5RS6HEWBTYN4SHCVPQNP12X), air systems; revisit the shared quantity and fluid-balance Records.

Volumetric flow Q has units m³/s; average normal velocity over a section is Q/A only when A is the actual flowing area. Air-change rate is Q/V, often expressed per hour. It needs a declared flow: total supply, outdoor air or a particular effective removal flow.

For hypothetical V=100 m³ and total supply Q=5 m³/s, Q/V=0.05 s⁻¹=180 h⁻¹. Over 20 m² this corresponds to an area-average 0.25 m/s, not a measured velocity at every work surface. A high exchange rate can coexist with bypass or stagnant regions.

**Work:** Convert between all three quantities for two geometries with the same room volume. State the area and flow basis each time. Explain why an exchange-rate target alone cannot determine local particle trajectories, outdoor-air provision or protection during movement.

